# The join a round-by-round preservation composes over, and the layers that
# guard it.
#
# Round-by-round soundness is defined over a state function on transcripts,
# so composing two such judgments is sound when the premise's doomed state
# is the conclusion's doomed state at the join. The premise's final round
# names that state -- claim_unsatisfied(c) -- and the conclusion's
# occurrence consumes an ordered claim vector; the hypothesis is that c is
# in it. The body states that now instead of ordering two spans and
# trusting the connection to have been made elsewhere.
#
# Three layers guard it, and this test pins which one answers, because the
# answer moved while the check was being written:
#
#   1. the binding's `SubjectRelation` -- both shipped preservation
#      bindings relate their premise by `consumed_claim`;
#   2. `apply.coverage` -- every produced consumed claim of the occurrence
#      must be selected by some premise relation, checked per application;
#   3. the body's own join -- the claim the premise's final round leaves
#      unsatisfied is consumed here, and a premise that names no state at
#      all cannot be placed.
#
# Layer 3 is not redundant with layer 2 even though layer 2 answers the
# case below: a premise derived at a path occurrence carries rounds with no
# state predicate, and the composition must refuse it rather than fall back
# to the positional proxy. Nothing in the tree builds that derivation yet,
# which is why the reachable half is pinned here and the rest is a refusal
# the body owns.

# RUN: rm -rf %t && mkdir -p %t
# RUN: zkc-seal %S/../Evidence/Inputs/gkr-width2-depth3.mlir %zkc-seal-full -o %t/chain.artifacts > /dev/null
# RUN: %python %S/../SemanticClosure/lib/artifact_handoff.py stage %t/chain.artifacts %t/chain.mlirbc

# The shipped signature: the chain composes, nine rounds across three
# layers, each round carrying the claim its occurrence argues about.
# RUN: zkc-derive --signature=%zkc-registry-dir/soundness-signature.json \
# RUN:   --protocol-vocabulary=%zkc-registry-dir/protocol-vocabulary.json \
# RUN:   --construction-profile-registry=%zkc-registry-dir/construction-profiles.json \
# RUN:   --request=%S/../Evidence/Inputs/gkr-width2-depth3-chain-request.json \
# RUN:   -o=%t/witness.json %t/chain.mlirbc
# RUN: FileCheck %s --check-prefix=BOUND < %t/witness.json
# BOUND: "round_index": "3:2"
# BOUND-NEXT: "state_predicate"
# BOUND: "claim_unsatisfied"

# The same signature with one preservation binding relating its premise by
# `same_subject` instead. The relation is admitted and the signature loads;
# what refuses is the coverage layer, which is the earlier of the two gates
# that stand between a loose relation and a composition without a
# claim-graph connection.
# RUN: %python %S/Inputs/loosen_preservation_relation.py \
# RUN:   %zkc-registry-dir/soundness-signature.json %t/loosened.json
# RUN: not zkc-derive --signature=%t/loosened.json \
# RUN:   --protocol-vocabulary=%zkc-registry-dir/protocol-vocabulary.json \
# RUN:   --construction-profile-registry=%zkc-registry-dir/construction-profiles.json \
# RUN:   --request=%S/../Evidence/Inputs/gkr-width2-depth3-chain-request.json \
# RUN:   -o=/dev/null %t/chain.mlirbc 2>&1 | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=LOOSE
# LOOSE: coverage_mismatch at apply.coverage
# LOOSE-SAME: do not follow the claim graph
